Cor Pulmonale
Respiratory System
Elevated JVP, Peripheral edema, Right ventricular heave

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-013
Reason for Visit: Worsening severe leg swelling, extreme fatigue, and exertional syncope in a patient with chronic lung disease, requiring urgent cardiovascular evaluation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported new-onset syncopal episodes upon exertion alongside massive lower limb edema. Urgent assessment was necessary to differentiate acute right ventricular failure from massive pulmonary embolism prior to the regular in-hours clinic opening.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My legs are swollen like tree trunks, my belly aches, and I passed out just trying to walk to the kitchen.
History of Present Illness: The patient describes progressive exertional dyspnea out of proportion to their known lung function decline over the past few months. In the last week, they developed severe bilateral pitting peripheral edema and abdominal bloating. The patient reports severe chronic fatigue and two episodes of exertional syncope in the past 48 hours. They have known chronic hypoxemia but admit to poor compliance with their home oxygen therapy.
Functional Status: Highly restricted; currently unable to walk more than a few steps without severe dyspnea and dizziness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; extremely high risk for falls secondary to syncope.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/60 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 22 breaths/min | SpO2: 85% on room air | Wt: 95 Kg (up 5kg this week)
General: Chronically unwell, cyanosed lips, visibly distended abdomen, laboured breathing at rest.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals an elevated jugular venous pressure (JVP) with prominent waves. Palpation of the chest demonstrates a distinct right ventricular heave. Auscultation reveals a loud pulmonary component of the second heart sound (loud P2) and a pansystolic murmur consistent with tricuspid regurgitation. Abdominal exam shows tender hepatomegaly and mild ascites. Lower extremities exhibit 3+ pitting edema up to the thighs.
Environmental Observations: Home oxygen concentrator is turned off in the corner of the room.
Medication Reconciliation: Diuretics were previously prescribed but ceased by the patient due to frequent urination.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Cor Pulmonale - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis (Bilateral) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of elevated JVP, tender hepatomegaly, and right ventricular heave, indicating a central cardiac cause rather than peripheral venous occlusion.

Liver Cirrhosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound exertional dyspnea, right heart signs, and elevated JVP point to cardiac rather than primary hepatic pathology.

Left-Sided Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear lung fields (absence of pulmonary edema crackles) despite massive systemic fluid overload.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents in acute decompensated right heart failure (cor pulmonale) secondary to chronic unmanaged hypoxemia. The classic tetrad of elevated JVP, peripheral edema, right ventricular heave, and tender hepatomegaly is overtly present. The recent exertional syncope episodes are a critical red flag indicating fixed right ventricular output and severe pulmonary hypertension. Deferring this assessment until the next in-hours period carried an unacceptable risk of sudden cardiac death or irreversible end-organ damage from venous congestion. An urgent after-hours assessment was strictly mandated to reinstitute continuous oxygen therapy, administer potent loop diuretics, and initiate hospital transfer protocols.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IV Furosemide 40mg immediately. Reinstated continuous oxygen therapy at 2L/min to reverse hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction. Initiated urgent ambulance transfer to the Emergency Department for comprehensive right heart assessment and diuresis.
Positive Behavioral Change: Weigh yourself every single morning after using the toilet; seeing the number drop on the scale provides immediate visible proof that the fluid is leaving your body, reinforcing the daily use of your water tablets.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable; hospital admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover provided to the Emergency Department admitting team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is being transferred to the Emergency Department immediately due to the high risk of recurrent syncope and severe fluid overload.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Formal diagnosis of the severity of pulmonary hypertension requires an echocardiogram in the hospital.
Clinical Course: You require intravenous medication in the hospital to rapidly remove the dangerous amount of fluid stressing your heart and liver.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk or stand up while waiting for the ambulance; doing so could cause you to faint again due to the strain on your right heart.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Because you stopped your oxygen, the blood vessels in your lungs clamped shut, causing massive back-pressure on the right side of your heart; this back-pressure forced fluid into your liver and legs.
Actionable Steps: You must wear your oxygen constantly exactly as prescribed; the oxygen directly relaxes the blood vessels in your lungs, removing the dangerous pressure that is currently crushing your heart.

